    $l13_master$> For five chapters the question was *where* things can go in space — the ellipse, the perturbation, the rendezvous, the interplanetary transfer, the Lagrange point. This chapter asks the question that closes the arc and turns it from celestial mechanics toward human consequence: what happens when too many things go to the same place, and no one owns the place? Low Earth orbit is a commons — a shared, finite, un-owned resource — and it is filling with debris moving at speeds where a fleck of paint hits like a bullet and a bolt hits like a bomb. Here is the Kessler syndrome, the nightmare scenario in which collisions beget fragments that cause more collisions in a runaway chain that could render whole bands of orbit unusable for *generations* — a self-sustaining cascade that, once triggered, no one can stop. And it turns out this is not primarily a physics problem but a governance problem: a textbook tragedy of the commons, where each actor's individually rational choice (launch now, don't pay to deorbit, don't clean up others' mess) sums to a collectively catastrophic outcome, in a domain with no owner, no police, no property rights, and a body of space law written in 1967 for a world of two spacefaring superpowers, not tens of thousands of commercial satellites. The physics of orbits meets the economics of the commons and the law of the sky — and the collision, literal and figurative, is the subject. This is where the story of orbits stops being about *how* to use space and starts being about whether we can share it.

## The day two satellites hit at eleven kilometers per second

{{image: Space debris | The cloud of tracked objects around Earth — satellites and the debris of collisions past. The 2009 Iridium–Cosmos smash alone added ~2,000 trackable fragments to one of the most useful bands of low orbit, and they are still up there.}}

On February 10, 2009, at 16:56 UTC, 789 kilometers above the Taymyr Peninsula in northern Siberia, two satellites occupied the same point in space at the same instant. One was **Iridium 33**, an active American communications satellite, part of the Iridium constellation, doing its job, relaying calls. The other was **Cosmos 2251**, a defunct Russian military communications satellite, dead since 1995, a two-thirds-of-a-tonne hunk of tumbling metal that no one was controlling and no one was tracking closely enough to worry about. They were in nearly perpendicular orbits, and they met at a closing speed of about **11.7 kilometers per second** — roughly 42,000 kilometers per hour, more than ten times the speed of a rifle bullet.

There was no explosion in the chemical sense. There didn't need to be. At 11.7 km/s, kinetic energy alone is apocalyptic: the two satellites, with a combined mass of about 1.5 tonnes, carried the kinetic energy of a large truck bomb, and when they met, they were not dented or cracked but *vaporized and shattered* — converted in a fraction of a second into an expanding cloud of debris. The two satellites became roughly **2,000 trackable fragments** (pieces larger than about 10 cm) and many hundreds of thousands of smaller pieces too small to track but each still lethal. This was the first-ever accidental hypervelocity collision between two intact satellites, and it happened not because anyone was careless in the moment but because two objects were in orbits that happened to intersect, in a sky where nobody had the authority or the complete information to prevent it.

Here is the fact to sit with, because it contains the whole chapter. **The debris from that 2009 collision is still up there.** At 789 km altitude, atmospheric drag is so feeble that these fragments will take *decades to centuries* to decay — many of them will still be circling the Earth, at 7.5 km/s, when your grandchildren are old. Each fragment is now an independent hazard, on its own orbit, crossing the paths of working satellites thousands of times a day. And every one of those fragments can *cause the next collision* — a 10-cm piece of a dead Russian satellite striking a working satellite would shatter *it* too, creating thousands more fragments, each of which could cause further collisions. The 2009 event didn't just destroy two satellites; it permanently raised the debris density in one of the most useful bands of low Earth orbit, and every satellite that flies through that band forever after runs a slightly higher risk of being the next victim, and the next source.

Two years earlier, in January 2007, something worse had happened *on purpose.* China tested an anti-satellite weapon by deliberately ramming a missile into one of its own defunct weather satellites, **Fengyun-1C**, at about 865 km. That single intentional act created more than **3,000 trackable fragments** — the largest debris-generating event in history — a cloud that spread into a shell around the entire planet at an altitude crowded with working satellites, and that will persist for *centuries.* One political decision, one missile, and a band of orbit was polluted for longer than any human institution has ever lasted.

**These are not freak accidents. They are the visible early symptoms of a structural condition:** low Earth orbit is a shared resource, finite and precious, filling with lethal debris that no one is obligated to clean up, moving at speeds where the smallest fragment is a weapon and the largest collision is irreversible, governed by no one with the power to stop it. The 2009 collision and the 2007 test are what the tragedy of the commons looks like when the commons is 800 kilometers up and the sheep are moving at eleven kilometers per second.

A word on why any of this belongs in a study of astrodynamics at all. You might think debris and law are someone else's department — policy people, lawyers, not the engineers who compute trajectories. That view is exactly the problem. **The Kessler syndrome is caused by orbital mechanics and can only be understood through it** — why debris persists, why collisions cascade, why some orbits are self-cleaning and others are not: all of it is drag and orbits and relative velocities. And the *solutions* — deorbit burns, graveyard orbits, collision avoidance, active debris removal — are all astrodynamics problems. The engineer who designs a satellite decides, in that design, whether it will become multi-generational debris or clean up after itself. **Governance of the commons is not separate from the engineering; it is a consequence of ten thousand engineering choices**, and engineers are the ones making them. Spaceflight is done by human institutions, and a spacecraft engineer who cannot reason about incentives and governance is only half-educated. This chapter is where the physics acquires a conscience. It begins with why a fleck of paint can kill.

## Why a fleck of paint is a bullet: the physics of hypervelocity debris

Before the syndrome and the economics, we need the physics of *why orbital debris is so uniquely dangerous*, because the danger is not obvious and it drives everything. The key is a number you already own from the two-body chapter: **orbital velocity in low Earth orbit is about 7.5 km/s.** Everything up there — every satellite, every spent bolt, every fleck of paint — is moving at roughly this speed, and when two things on different orbits meet, they meet at a *relative* speed that can be anywhere from a gentle closing rate up to nearly **twice** orbital speed (~15 km/s) for a head-on encounter, with a typical collision around **10 km/s.** This is *hypervelocity* — far faster than any bullet, faster than almost anything in human experience — and at these speeds, the physics of impact is not like being hit by a fast object. It's like an explosion.

### Kinetic energy at hypervelocity

Kinetic energy scales as the *square* of speed ($KE = \frac{1}{2}mv^2$), and orbital speeds are so high that even tiny masses carry enormous energy. Some numbers you can feel:

- **A 1-gram fleck** (a paint chip, a metal sliver) at 10 km/s carries 50 kJ — about **12 grams of TNT equivalent**, and more kinetic energy than a hand grenade's fragment.
- **A 10-gram bolt** at 10 km/s carries 500 kJ — **over 300 times the kinetic energy of a high-powered rifle bullet.** A single lost bolt, in orbit, is a burst of energy that would punch clean through a spacecraft wall and everything behind it.
- **A 1-kilogram fragment** at 10 km/s carries 50 MJ — about **12 kg of TNT**, enough to obliterate any satellite it hits.

This is why orbital debris is categorically different from litter. On Earth, a dropped bolt is trash. In orbit, a dropped bolt is a **hypervelocity projectile with the energy of a small bomb**, and it stays that way for decades or centuries. The famous, almost unbelievable fact: a **fleck of paint** traveling at orbital speed has chipped and cracked the windows of the Space Shuttle and the International Space Station on multiple occasions — a *paint chip*, gouging a crater in reinforced spacecraft glass, because at 10 km/s a paint chip hits with the energy of a bullet. The ISS regularly performs maneuvers to dodge tracked debris, and its hull is armored (Whipple shields — spaced layers that shatter and disperse an incoming fragment before it penetrates) specifically against the untrackable small stuff.

```example
debris-energy
```

The relative velocity matters as much as the mass, and it is set by geometry. Two objects can both move at 7.5 km/s yet close at very different speeds depending on the angle at which their orbits cross: near zero if they travel together, $\sqrt{2}$ times orbital speed at a right angle, and up to *twice* orbital speed head-on. The closing speed is the vector difference $2v\sin(\theta/2)$ — the very same formula as the plane-change penalty, geometry rotating a velocity vector.

```example
debris-closing-speed
```

### The population: what's up there

The debris environment, as of the mid-2020s, is roughly:

- **~35,000–40,000 objects larger than 10 cm** — these are *tracked* by ground radar and catalogued, each one a known hazard whose orbit is followed. Only a few thousand of these are working satellites; the rest are dead satellites, spent rocket stages, and collision fragments.
- **~1 million objects between 1 and 10 cm** — too small to track reliably, too large to shield against. This is the **most dangerous population**: each one is lethal to a satellite (a 1-cm fragment at 10 km/s carries the energy of a hand grenade), and we cannot see them coming. You cannot dodge what you cannot track.
- **~130 million objects larger than 1 mm** — untrackable, individually able to damage or degrade a spacecraft, collectively a constant sandblasting of everything in orbit.

And the number is *growing*, both from new launches (the megaconstellations — Starlink alone has launched thousands of satellites, with tens of thousands more planned across several operators) and from collisions and breakups that multiply existing debris. Every object up there is both a *potential victim* (it can be destroyed by an impact) and a *potential source* (when destroyed, it becomes thousands of new fragments). This dual nature — victim and source — is the seed of the catastrophe, because it means debris can *reproduce.* And a hazard that reproduces is a hazard that can run away. Which brings us to Kessler.

## The Kessler syndrome: when collisions become self-sustaining

{{image: Kessler syndrome | The Kessler syndrome: past a critical density, collisions create fragments faster than drag removes them, so the debris population runs away on its own — a chain reaction with no control rods, like an epidemic with R₀ > 1.}}

In 1978, a NASA scientist named **Donald Kessler**, together with Burton Cour-Palais, published a paper with a quietly terrifying thesis. They pointed out that as the number of objects in low Earth orbit grows, the rate of collisions between them grows too — and not linearly, but roughly with the *square* of the population (because collisions require pairs, and the number of possible pairs grows as $n^2$). Each collision produces fragments, which *add* to the population, which *increases* the collision rate further. And they identified the threshold condition that turns this into a nightmare: **once the debris density in some band of orbit exceeds a critical value, the collisions become self-sustaining** — the fragments from collisions cause new collisions faster than atmospheric drag removes debris, and the population grows *on its own*, without any new launches, in a runaway chain reaction that feeds on itself. This is the **Kessler syndrome** (or "collisional cascading"), and it is the central nightmare of spaceflight's future.

### The cascade logic

The runaway logic, step by step — its structure is the same as a nuclear chain reaction or an epidemic, and recognizing that structure is the point:

1. A collision (or explosion, or anti-satellite test) creates a cloud of fragments.
2. Each fragment is on its own orbit, crossing the paths of other objects at hypervelocity.
3. Some of those fragments strike other satellites or debris.
4. Each strike shatters its target into *thousands* more fragments.
5. Those new fragments cause more strikes, creating more fragments, causing more strikes...

The critical quantity is whether, on average, each collision causes *more than one* subsequent collision before its fragments decay. This is exactly the **basic reproduction number** $R_0$ of an epidemic. If each collision produces, on average, fewer than one further collision ($R_0 < 1$), the process fizzles out — debris is created but drag removes it faster than it cascades. If each collision produces *more* than one further collision ($R_0 > 1$), the process **runs away** — a self-sustaining chain reaction, the Kessler syndrome, where the debris population grows exponentially on its own, fed by its own fragments, until the orbit is so full of debris that nothing can survive there.

```example
collision-pairs
```

**And here is the truly frightening part: once triggered, it cannot be stopped.** A nuclear chain reaction can be damped with control rods; an epidemic can be slowed with vaccines and distancing. But a Kessler cascade in a high-altitude orbit has no brake. You cannot recall the fragments. You cannot stop them from colliding. Atmospheric drag, the only natural cleanup mechanism, is negligible at the altitudes where the cascade is most dangerous (800–1200 km — where drag lifetimes are *centuries to millennia*). So a cascade at those altitudes would run for *generations*, and there is nothing anyone could do but watch. The orbit would fill with a shell of debris and become unusable — not for years, but for **centuries** — a band of near-Earth space rendered off-limits to all of humanity, possibly permanently on any timescale that matters.

### Why altitude is everything

Notice the crucial role of altitude, which ties the whole thing back to atmospheric drag. At **low** altitudes (below ~600 km), drag is strong enough to deorbit debris within years to a couple of decades — so even if collisions happen, the fragments *decay away* before they can cascade, and $R_0$ stays low. The atmosphere is a free, natural debris-removal system down low. This is why the ISS (~400 km) sits in a relatively self-cleaning zone. But at **higher** altitudes (800–1200 km — which is, unfortunately, a hugely popular band for sun-synchronous imaging satellites, communications, and now megaconstellations), drag is negligible and debris persists for centuries to millennia. There is no natural cleanup. So this is exactly where the Kessler syndrome is most dangerous, and — cruel irony — exactly where the most valuable satellites live. **The orbits we most want to use are the ones most vulnerable to permanent debris catastrophe, because they're too high for the atmosphere to clean.** Drag, which was a killer in the decay chapter (it deorbited Skylab), is here a *savior* — the only thing standing between us and permanent cascade — and it only works down low. Above its reach, the commons has no janitor.

See the cruelty for yourself. Slide the altitude below: down at the ISS, drag clears debris in a couple of years; but in the 800–1200 km band — exactly where the imaging, communications, and megaconstellation satellites crowd — the lifetime is centuries to millennia, and there is no janitor.

```debrislifetime
```

### Are we there yet?

Is the Kessler syndrome already happening? The honest answer is: we're at the edge, and parts of it may have already begun. Several studies (including by Kessler himself in later years) concluded that certain altitude bands, particularly around 800–1000 km, may *already* be above the critical density — meaning the cascade is, slowly, self-sustaining there even now, and would continue to grow even if all launches stopped tomorrow. The 2007 Chinese anti-satellite test and the 2009 Iridium-Cosmos collision each substantially increased the debris in these bands and may have nudged them past threshold. The cascade, if it has begun, is *slow* — it plays out over decades, not days, so there's no dramatic moment — but slowness is not safety; it's just a catastrophe you can't see happening in real time. We are, quite possibly, living in the early decades of a Kessler cascade, and the question is no longer purely "will it start?" but "how bad will we let it get, and can we reverse it?" And *that* question is not physics. It's economics and law. Which is where the real difficulty lives.

## The mitigation toolkit: what engineering can do

Before turning to why the problem is hard to *solve* (economics and law), consider what the *engineering* can do, because the tools exist and they're all applications of orbital mechanics. The problem is not that we don't know how to mitigate debris; it's that knowing how and being obligated to are different things.

**1. Deorbit at end of life (use drag, or a disposal burn).** A satellite in low orbit should, at end of mission, be brought down to burn up in the atmosphere. If it's low enough, you just let drag do it — but international guidelines require this happen within 25 years (recently tightened toward 5 years in the US), and above ~600 km natural drag is too slow, so the satellite must *actively* lower itself with a disposal burn while it still has propellant. This is why responsible operators reserve a final slug of propellant for a deorbit maneuver — a small Δv (a few tens to hundreds of m/s to drop perigee into the atmosphere) that turns the satellite from eternal debris into a harmless streak in the sky. The catch: it costs propellant (hence payload, hence money), and it requires the satellite to still be functional at end of life (a satellite that dies suddenly can't deorbit itself — like Cosmos 2251, which died in 1995 and became the 2009 collision partner).

**2. Graveyard orbits (for high orbits you can't deorbit).** For geostationary satellites (35,786 km), deorbiting is prohibitively expensive — you'd need enormous Δv to drop from GEO into the atmosphere. So instead, GEO satellites at end of life are boosted *up* a few hundred km into a **graveyard orbit** above the GEO belt, getting them out of the crowded operational ring. This is cheaper (a small Δv up rather than a huge Δv down) but it's not *cleanup* — it's just moving the junk to a less-used shelf. It works for now because there's no drag up there to cascade the graveyard, but it's a stopgap, not a solution: the graveyard fills up too, eventually.

**3. Passivation (don't explode).** A major source of debris is *explosions* of dead satellites and spent rocket stages — leftover propellant, pressurized tanks, and charged batteries can rupture years after a mission ends, spontaneously fragmenting a dead object into thousands of pieces. **Passivation** means venting leftover propellant, depressurizing tanks, and discharging batteries at end of life, so the dead object can't later explode. It's cheap, it's now standard practice, and it prevents a whole category of debris-creation. (Many of the objects up there are old rocket upper stages that were left pressurized and later blew up.)

**4. Collision avoidance (dodge what you can track).** Active satellites with propulsion can *maneuver* to dodge tracked debris. Ground-based radar tracks the ~40,000 large objects, predicts close approaches ("conjunctions"), and operators fire thrusters to move out of the way. The ISS does this several times a year; Starlink satellites do it autonomously and constantly. But this only works against *tracked* debris (>10 cm), does nothing against the lethal 1–10 cm population you can't see, and costs propellant and operational disruption every time. And as the number of objects grows, the number of conjunction warnings grows as $n^2$ — operators are already drowning in alerts.

**5. Active debris removal (go up and grab the junk).** The hardest and most necessary: actually removing existing debris — sending a spacecraft to rendezvous with a dead satellite or spent stage and drag it down to burn up, using nets, harpoons, robotic arms, or attaching a deorbit motor. Several missions have demonstrated pieces of this (ESA's ClearSpace, various commercial efforts, Japanese tether experiments). But it's *expensive*, technically hard (grabbing a tumbling, uncooperative multi-tonne object at orbital speed is a serious rendezvous problem), and — the killer question — **who pays to remove debris that someone else created?** The engineering is tractable; the economics is not, and that's the whole problem.

Notice that every one of these is an astrodynamics problem — deorbit burns and graveyard boosts are Δv budgets, collision avoidance is conjunction prediction and maneuvering, active removal is rendezvous with an uncooperative target. **The engineer designing a satellite chooses, in that design, whether it will passivate, whether it reserves deorbit propellant, whether it can maneuver.** The tools all exist. So why is the sky filling with junk? Because knowing how to prevent it and being *obligated and incentivized* to prevent it are entirely different things — and the gap between them is a problem that physics cannot solve. It's a problem in the structure of the commons.

## The tragedy of the commons, at eleven kilometers per second

Now we arrive at the intellectual heart of the chapter, and it's not physics — it's economics and game theory. The reason low Earth orbit is filling with debris despite our knowing exactly how to prevent it is a structural problem that has a name and a long history: the **tragedy of the commons.**

### The classic tragedy

In 1968, ecologist Garrett Hardin published an essay describing a scenario: a common pasture, open to all herders, on which anyone may graze cattle. Each herder, acting rationally in their own self-interest, reasons as follows: "If I add one more cow to the common pasture, *I* get the full benefit of that cow (its milk, its meat), but the *cost* — the slight overgrazing, the degradation of the shared pasture — is spread across *all* the herders, so I bear only a tiny fraction of it. Therefore, it's rational for me to add another cow. And another." Every herder reasons identically, every herder adds cows, and the pasture — a shared, finite resource with no owner to protect it — is overgrazed into ruin, destroyed for everyone. **Each actor, behaving perfectly rationally for themselves, contributes to a collectively catastrophic outcome that no one wanted.** That is the tragedy: not that people are stupid or evil, but that individually rational choices sum to collective disaster when a finite resource is shared with no owner and no rules.

### Orbit is the pasture

Low Earth orbit is the common pasture, and the structure maps exactly, chillingly:

- **The resource is shared and finite:** usable orbital "slots" and altitude bands are limited, and the debris environment is a shared condition that affects everyone who uses the orbit.
- **The benefit of using it is private:** if I launch a satellite, *I* get all the revenue and capability from it.
- **The cost of degrading it is shared:** if my satellite adds to the debris risk (by not deorbiting, by risking collision, by fragmenting), that cost — the increased danger to *everyone's* satellites, the erosion of the commons toward Kessler — is spread across all users, so I bear only a tiny fraction of it.
- **Therefore each actor is individually incentivized to over-use and under-protect:** launch more, spend less on deorbit and passivation, don't pay to clean up. Each of these is individually rational (saves *me* money, gives *me* capability) and collectively catastrophic (degrades the commons toward permanent cascade).

**This is why the sky is filling with junk despite everyone knowing how to prevent it.** Deorbiting your satellite costs *you* propellant and payload; the benefit of a cleaner orbit accrues to *everyone.* Passivating, reserving deorbit fuel, removing your dead satellites — all cost the individual operator and benefit the collective. So the rational individual operator underinvests in protection, and the sum of all those rational underinvestments is a degrading commons sliding toward Kessler. No one is the villain. The 2009 collision wasn't caused by a bad actor; it was caused by a *structure* in which no one was obligated to remove Cosmos 2251, so no one did, and it sat there for fourteen years until it hit something. **The tragedy of the orbital commons is not a failure of individuals; it is a failure of structure — of a valuable, finite, shared resource with no owner, no property rights, no rules with teeth, and no one empowered to protect it.**

### The debris externality

In the language of economics, debris is a **negative externality** — a cost that an actor imposes on others but doesn't have to pay themselves. When you launch a satellite and don't deorbit it, you impose a cost (increased collision risk, degraded commons) on every other orbit-user, but that cost doesn't appear on *your* balance sheet. Because you don't pay it, you don't factor it into your decisions — you "over-produce" debris relative to what would be socially optimal. This is the same structure as pollution: a factory that dumps waste into a river gets the private benefit of cheap disposal while imposing the cost (a fouled river) on everyone downstream. Debris is orbital pollution, and it has exactly the economic pathology of all pollution: **the polluter doesn't pay, so there's too much pollution.**

The classic economic fixes for externalities — and their difficulties in orbit — come shortly. But hold onto the core diagnosis, because it reframes everything: **the debris crisis is not fundamentally a technology problem (we have the mitigation tools) or a knowledge problem (we understand the physics). It is an incentive problem — a mismatch between who benefits from using the commons and who pays for degrading it.** And incentive problems are solved not by engineering but by governance: by rules, property rights, prices, treaties, enforcement. Which, in orbit, we mostly do not have.

## The law of the sky: a treaty from 1967 and its discontents

So the debris crisis is an incentive problem, and incentive problems are solved by governance. What governance do we have for space? The answer is: a remarkable, idealistic, and increasingly inadequate body of law written for a world that no longer exists.

{{image: Outer Space Treaty | The Outer Space Treaty of 1967 — visionary for its era, and the root of the tragedy: by declaring space "the province of all mankind" with no national appropriation, it made orbit a thing no one owns, and a thing no one owns is a thing no one protects.}}

### The Outer Space Treaty

The foundational document is the **Outer Space Treaty of 1967** — formally the "Treaty on Principles Governing the Activities of States in the Exploration and Use of Outer Space." It was negotiated at the height of the Cold War, when spaceflight was the exclusive province of two superpowers (the US and USSR), and it is a genuinely visionary document. Its core principles:

- **Space is free for exploration and use by all nations** — no country may claim territory in space.
- **No national appropriation:** you cannot plant a flag on the Moon and claim it, the way European powers claimed continents. Space (including the Moon and other bodies) is not subject to national sovereignty. It is, in the treaty's spirit, the **"province of all mankind."**
- **No weapons of mass destruction in orbit** (no nukes in space).
- **States bear responsibility for their national space activities** — including activities by their private companies — and are **liable for damage** their space objects cause.

That last point matters for debris: under the treaty and the related 1972 **Liability Convention**, a country is liable for damage caused by its space objects. In principle, if your satellite hits mine, your country pays. In practice, this has almost never been invoked (the 2009 Iridium-Cosmos collision produced no liability claim — proving fault and causation for a hypervelocity collision between a working and a dead satellite is a legal nightmare, and neither side wanted the precedent), and it does nothing about the vast majority of debris, which is un-tracked, un-attributed, and causes gradual degradation rather than a single provable hit.

### Why the 1967 framework is inadequate today

The Outer Space Treaty is a beautiful document, and it was adequate for its era. But it was written for **two spacefaring superpowers**, and we now live in a world of **dozens of spacefaring nations and thousands of commercial operators**, launching tens of thousands of satellites. The mismatch is structural:

- **It establishes no property rights.** Because "no national appropriation" was meant to prevent colonial land-grabs, orbit is nobody's property — which sounds noble but is *exactly* the tragedy-of-the-commons condition. There are no orbital "slots" you own, no altitude bands you're responsible for, no mechanism to price the use of the commons. The very anti-appropriation principle that prevents a superpower from claiming the Moon also prevents any system of orbital property rights that might align incentives. **The treaty's greatest virtue (no one owns space) is also the root of the tragedy (no one owns space, so no one protects it).**

- **It has weak enforcement.** The treaty relies on states policing their own activities and on liability that's nearly impossible to invoke. There is no space traffic controller with authority, no orbital police, no binding debris limits with penalties. The debris-mitigation "guidelines" (the 25-year deorbit rule, passivation) are exactly that — *guidelines*, voluntary best practices, not enforceable law. Compliance is incomplete and unenforceable.

- **It didn't anticipate the commercial explosion or megaconstellations.** The drafters imagined a handful of government satellites, not a company launching 42,000 satellites in a single constellation. The regulatory framework for who may launch what, into which orbits, with what debris obligations, is being improvised nation by nation (mostly through national launch licensing) with no global coordination.

- **The anti-satellite problem.** Nothing in the treaty prohibits a nation from destroying its *own* satellite with a missile (as China did in 2007, and Russia did in 2021, and the US and India have done), even though such tests create catastrophic, long-lived debris that endangers everyone. Anti-satellite weapon tests are a debris-generating act of the worst kind, and they are essentially unregulated. (There is a growing movement for a voluntary test moratorium, which the US and several nations have adopted unilaterally — a hopeful sign, but non-binding.)

### The governance gap

So we have a governance gap of exactly the kind that makes tragedies of the commons intractable: a valuable shared resource, degrading through the summed rational choices of many actors, governed by a well-meaning but toothless framework with no property rights, no enforcement, no pricing of the externality, and no global authority. The engineering solutions exist but aren't mandated; the economic diagnosis is clear but the fixes require governance we don't have; and the legal framework is a Cold War relic straining under a commercial reality it never imagined. **This is the hardest kind of problem — not because any piece is unsolvable, but because the solution requires collective action among sovereign, competing actors with no one empowered to compel them.** It is, in miniature and in orbit, the same structure as climate change, fisheries collapse, and every other global-commons tragedy: everyone would be better off if everyone cooperated, and everyone is individually tempted to defect, and there's no world government to enforce cooperation.

## Is the tragedy inevitable? Ostrom's rebuttal and the paths out

This is not a counsel of despair, because the tragedy of the commons is *not* a law of nature, and the most important intellectual development in this field is the reason why. When Garrett Hardin published his 1968 essay, its bleak conclusion — commons are doomed without either privatization or top-down government control — became conventional wisdom. But it was **challenged, and substantially overturned**, by the political economist **Elinor Ostrom**, whose work on how real communities actually govern shared resources — collected in her landmark study {{book: Elinor Ostrom | Governing the Commons | 1990}} — won her the 2009 Nobel Prize in Economics (the first woman to win it).

### Ostrom's finding: commons can be governed

Ostrom studied real commons all over the world — fisheries, forests, irrigation systems, grazing lands — and found that Hardin was *empirically wrong* in many cases: communities frequently *do* successfully manage shared resources without either privatizing them or imposing external government control, through **self-organized governance** — locally-agreed rules, monitoring, graduated sanctions for violators, and mechanisms for collective decision-making. She identified design principles for successful commons governance: clearly defined boundaries (who may use the resource), rules matched to local conditions, collective choice arrangements (users participate in making the rules), monitoring, graduated sanctions, conflict-resolution mechanisms, and recognition of the community's right to organize. **The tragedy of the commons is not inevitable; it is the outcome of a *particular* institutional failure, and other institutions can avert it.**

This is genuinely hopeful for orbit, and it reframes the challenge: the question is not "can a commons be saved?" (Ostrom showed it can) but "can we build the *institutions* — the rules, monitoring, and sanctions — to govern the orbital commons before it cascades?" The orbital commons is a hard case for Ostrom's principles (the "community" is sovereign nations and competing corporations, boundaries are ill-defined, monitoring is technically hard, and there's no shared enforcement) — but it's not hopeless, and her work tells us where to look: not (only) at technology, but at institution-building.

### The paths out

Several approaches, none sufficient alone, are being pursued or proposed:

- **Binding debris-mitigation rules with enforcement** — turning the voluntary 25-year (now 5-year) deorbit guidelines into enforceable requirements tied to launch licensing, so operators *must* deorbit or face penalties. National regulators (the FCC in the US) are beginning to do this, which is a form of governance through the leverage of launch licensing.

- **Pricing the externality** — charging operators for the debris risk they create (an "orbital use fee" proportional to the collision risk and persistence of their satellites), so the cost of degrading the commons appears on the operator's balance sheet and they're incentivized to deorbit promptly and choose safer orbits. This is the economist's classic fix for an externality (a Pigouvian tax), and studies suggest it could substantially improve outcomes — but it requires international agreement on who charges and collects.

- **Space traffic management** — a coordinated system (like air traffic control) that tracks all objects, predicts conjunctions, and coordinates maneuvers, ideally with some authority. This is being built piecemeal (the US Space Force's tracking, commercial services, national space situational awareness) but lacks global integration and authority.

- **Active debris removal, funded collectively** — since no individual operator will pay to remove debris they didn't create, active removal may need to be funded as a *public good*, collectively (by governments, or by a fee on all operators). The engineering exists; it needs a payer.

- **Anti-satellite test bans** — binding international prohibition of debris-generating anti-satellite tests, building on the voluntary moratoria several nations have adopted.

None of these is easy, and all require the collective action among competing sovereigns that makes commons problems hard. But they are *paths*, and Ostrom's work proves the paths can lead somewhere. The orbital commons can, in principle, be governed. Whether it *will* be, before a cascade forecloses the option, is one of the genuinely open and consequential questions of the coming decades — and it will be decided not by physicists but by the interaction of engineers, economists, lawyers, and diplomats. **Which is exactly why a spacecraft engineer needs to understand all of it.**

## Coda: the commons, and the close of the orbital arc

Collect the argument — of the chapter, and of the whole arc it closes.

**The story of orbits began with a single ellipse around a single planet, and it ends with the question of whether humanity can share the space around that planet at all.** That arc is not incidental; it's the deep structure of the subject. The preceding chapters taught, with growing power, *how to use space* — the two-body orbit, the tuned perturbation, the rendezvous, the interplanetary transfer, the Lagrange point. We became, chapter by chapter, masters of *where things can go.* And this chapter confronts the consequence of that mastery: **we have become so good at putting things in orbit that we are on the verge of ruining orbit for everyone**, and the tools that made us masters of trajectory are useless against a problem that is not about trajectory at all, but about incentives, institutions, and the governance of a shared thing with no owner.

**The physics of the crisis is pure orbital mechanics.** Debris is lethal because orbital velocity is 7.5 km/s and kinetic energy scales as velocity squared, so a fleck of paint hits like a bullet and a bolt like a bomb. Debris persists because drag is negligible above ~600 km — the atmosphere, our only free janitor, can't reach the orbits we most want. Collisions cascade because each fragment is both victim and source, and above the critical density the Kessler syndrome runs away like a chain reaction, unstoppable, for centuries. And the mitigation tools — deorbit burns, graveyard orbits, collision avoidance, active removal — are all astrodynamics. The engineer who understands orbits understands both the disease and the cure.

**But the disease has no engineering cure, because it isn't fundamentally an engineering problem.** It's a **tragedy of the commons**: a finite, shared, un-owned resource, degraded by the summed rational choices of many actors, each of whom captures the private benefit of using orbit while imposing the cost of degrading it on everyone. Debris is a negative externality — orbital pollution — and the polluter doesn't pay, so there's too much of it. We know how to prevent debris; we are not obligated or incentivized to. The gap between knowing-how and being-made-to is a governance gap, and the governance we have is a visionary 1967 treaty written for two superpowers, straining hopelessly under a commercial reality of thousands of operators, establishing no property rights, no enforcement, no pricing, no orbital police. **The treaty's noblest principle — that no one owns space — is the very root of the tragedy, because a thing no one owns is a thing no one protects.**

**And yet it is not hopeless.** Elinor Ostrom proved that the tragedy of the commons is not a law of nature but a *particular institutional failure*, and that real communities govern shared resources successfully through self-organized rules, monitoring, and sanctions. The orbital commons is a hard case — sovereign actors, ill-defined boundaries, difficult monitoring — but the paths exist: binding deorbit rules tied to launch licensing, pricing the debris externality, space traffic management, collectively-funded active removal, anti-satellite test bans. Whether we walk those paths before a cascade forecloses them is an open question, and it will be answered by engineers, economists, lawyers, and diplomats *together* — which is the deepest reason this subject belongs in an engineering education.

**Here is the thing to carry out of the whole study of orbits, and it's a widening of your sense of what an engineer is.** Through every previous chapter, the enemy was physics: the rocket equation, the gravity well, the unsolvable three-body problem — impersonal constraints that had to be understood and outwitted. This chapter introduces a different kind of constraint, one that will grow more important the more we do in space: the constraint of *sharing a finite commons with other humans who have their own interests.* This constraint is not solved by cleverness with equations. It is solved — if it is solved — by building institutions, aligning incentives, and achieving collective action, which is a harder and more human kind of engineering. **The spacecraft engineer of the coming decades cannot only be a master of trajectories; they must also be a citizen of the commons**, aware that every design choice (to passivate or not, to reserve deorbit fuel or not, to choose a self-cleaning orbit or a persistent one) is a small act of governance, a contribution to or a theft from a resource shared by all of humanity and by the generations to come. The orbit you fill is not yours. It is held in trust, and the trust is fraying, and repairing it is now part of the job.

**The through-line, one last time, transformed.** *Never carry anything through a Δv it does not need to experience* — the principle of not wasting what is scarce — reaches its final and largest form here: **never leave in orbit anything that does not need to be there.** The dead satellite, the spent stage, the un-deorbited hulk — these are the ultimate "dead mass carried through a Δv it doesn't need," except now the Δv is the endless coasting of debris through the shared sky, and the cost is borne not by one rocket's payload but by all of humanity's access to space. The discipline that began as *efficiency* (drop the dead tank) ends as *stewardship* (drop your dead satellite, don't foul the commons). It was always the same principle: don't burden the shared future with the dead weight of the careless present. At the start of the course it saved payload. Here, at the close of the orbital arc, it may save the sky.

**The arc is complete.** From Kepler's first exact ellipse to the governance of the orbital commons — from *where things can go* to *whether we can share where they go.* Along the way: the two-body foundation, the tuned perturbation, the counterintuitive rendezvous, the interplanetary reach, the unsolvable three-body frontier, and now the human commons. You can navigate the solar system, and you understand that navigating it well is not only a technical achievement but a moral and institutional one. **Next, the subject turns from motion to light** — from *where things go* to *how we see* — the atmosphere, the photon, and the great instruments that let a species bound to one small planet look all the way back to the first light in the universe. The orbits carried us; now we learn to see.

## Further reading

- **Donald J. Kessler & Burton G. Cour-Palais, "Collision Frequency of Artificial Satellites: The Creation of a Debris Belt" (1978).** The founding paper on collisional cascading — remarkably readable, and the origin of the syndrome that bears Kessler's name.
- **Garrett Hardin, "The Tragedy of the Commons" (1968).** The essay that named the structural problem at the heart of the debris crisis; short, provocative, and still argued over.
- **Elinor Ostrom, *Governing the Commons* (1990).** The Nobel-winning rebuttal to Hardin — how real communities govern shared resources without privatization or top-down control, and the design principles that make it work. Read it alongside Hardin: the two are in tension, and the tension is the point.
- **The Outer Space Treaty (1967).** The text itself is short and worth reading in full, together with any current summary of the international debris-mitigation guidelines.

## Problem set

*The orbital-commons set. Problems 1–3 are quantitative (debris physics, cascade logic, decay); 4–6 cover the economics and law; 7–10 are argumentative and synthetic. The essay problems have no single right answer — only well-argued and poorly-argued ones; what counts is the quality of the reasoning, not the conclusion. Full worked answers and sample essays follow.*

**1 — Why debris is lethal.**
**(a)** Compute the kinetic energy of a 5-gram debris fragment (a small bolt) striking a satellite at a relative velocity of 10 km/s. Express it in joules and in grams of TNT equivalent (1 g TNT = 4,184 J).
**(b)** Compare this to a rifle bullet (4 grams at 900 m/s). How many times more energy does the orbital fragment carry?
**(c)** Explain why the relative velocity in an orbital collision can be much higher than the orbital velocity itself, and estimate the maximum possible relative velocity for two objects in circular LEO.
**(d)** Explain why this makes even small, untrackable debris a serious hazard, and what a Whipple shield does about it.

**2 — The cascade condition.** The Kessler syndrome is a runaway cascade analogous to a chain reaction.
**(a)** Define the critical condition for a self-sustaining cascade in terms of the average number of subsequent collisions caused by each collision (analogous to an epidemic's $R_0$).
**(b)** Explain why the collision rate grows roughly as the *square* of the number of objects.
**(c)** Explain why the cascade, once triggered at high altitude, "cannot be stopped," and contrast this with a nuclear chain reaction (which can be damped).
**(d)** Why is drag central to whether a given altitude band is cascade-prone? Connect explicitly to atmospheric drag and orbital decay.

**3 — Altitude and decay.** Using what you know about atmospheric drag and decay lifetimes:
**(a)** Explain why debris at 400 km is far less of a long-term problem than debris at 1000 km, even though both are equally lethal in a collision.
**(b)** Rank these altitudes by how dangerous their debris is for *long-term* commons degradation: 350 km, 800 km, 1200 km, 36,000 km (GEO). Explain your ranking.
**(c)** Explain the cruel irony that the most valuable orbits (sun-synchronous imaging, ~800 km) are also the most vulnerable to permanent debris catastrophe.
**(d)** Why can't we rely on drag to clean up GEO, and what do we do instead?

**4 — The tragedy of the commons.**
**(a)** State the structure of the tragedy of the commons (Hardin) and map each element precisely onto the orbital debris problem: what is the shared resource, what is the private benefit, what is the shared cost?
**(b)** Explain why debris is a "negative externality" and why externalities lead to over-production of the harmful thing.
**(c)** Explain why "no one is the villain" in the 2009 Iridium-Cosmos collision — why the problem is structural, not a failure of individuals.
**(d)** Give one other real-world tragedy of the commons and explain the structural analogy to orbital debris.

**5 — Space law.**
**(a)** State the core principles of the 1967 Outer Space Treaty relevant to debris (non-appropriation, state responsibility, liability).
**(b)** Explain the paradox that the treaty's anti-appropriation principle (no one owns space) is both its noblest feature and the root of the commons tragedy.
**(c)** Why has the Liability Convention almost never been successfully invoked for debris, despite states being formally liable for their space objects?
**(d)** Identify three ways the 1967 framework is inadequate for the commercial, multi-actor reality of the 2020s.

**6 — Ostrom's rebuttal.**
**(a)** Explain how Elinor Ostrom challenged Hardin's conclusion that commons are doomed without privatization or top-down control.
**(b)** State three of Ostrom's design principles for successful commons governance, and assess how hard each is to apply to the orbital commons.
**(c)** Why is the orbital commons a particularly *hard* case for Ostrom-style self-governance? What makes it harder than, say, a village fishery?
**(d)** Does Ostrom's work make you more or less optimistic about the orbital commons? Defend your answer.

**7 — Design a debris-mitigation regime. (Essay.)** You are advising an international body on governing the orbital commons. Design a regime — combining engineering requirements, economic mechanisms, and legal/institutional structures — that you believe could realistically avert a Kessler cascade. Address: what is mandated, how it's enforced, how the externality is priced, who pays for cleanup, and how you achieve buy-in from competing sovereign actors. Be specific, and be honest about the weakest point of your proposal.

**8 — The anti-satellite problem. (Essay.)** Anti-satellite weapon tests (2007 China, 2021 Russia, and others) create catastrophic long-lived debris, yet are essentially unregulated because a nation destroying its *own* satellite violates no treaty.
**(a)** Explain why anti-satellite tests are among the worst debris-generating events.
**(b)** Argue for a binding international test ban — and then argue the strongest case *against* one (national security, verification, fairness to nations without the capability).
**(c)** Where do you come down, and why?

**9 — Is space law adequate? (Essay.)** Argue either that the existing legal framework (the Outer Space Treaty plus guidelines and national licensing) is fundamentally adequate and needs only better enforcement, OR that it is fundamentally inadequate and needs replacement or major augmentation. Steelman the opposing view before rejecting it.

**10 — Synthesis and finale. (Capstone essay.)** In four or five paragraphs, explain how this chapter closes the story of orbits and what the arc as a whole accomplished. Cover: the movement from "where things can go" (the ellipse, perturbations, rendezvous, interplanetary transfers, the three-body frontier) to "whether we can share where they go"; how the debris crisis is simultaneously a physics problem (orbital speed, drag, relative velocity) and a governance problem (economics, law); why the tragedy of the commons is the right frame; how Ostrom complicates the pessimism; and how the through-line ("never carry/leave what doesn't need to be there") transforms from efficiency into stewardship. Reflect, finally, on what it means that a study of astrodynamics ends not with an equation but with a question of collective human responsibility.

## Worked answers

### 1 — Why debris is lethal

**(a)** $KE = \frac{1}{2}mv^2 = \frac{1}{2}(0.005\ \text{kg})(10{,}000\ \text{m/s})^2 = \frac{1}{2}(0.005)(10^8) = \mathbf{250{,}000\ J = 250\ kJ}$. In TNT: $250{,}000/4{,}184 = \mathbf{59.8\ grams\ of\ TNT}$ — a 5-gram bolt carries the explosive-equivalent energy of about 60 grams of TNT.

**(b)** Rifle bullet: $KE = \frac{1}{2}(0.004)(900)^2 = 1{,}620\ J$. Ratio: $250{,}000/1{,}620 = \mathbf{154\ times}$ the energy of a rifle bullet. A small bolt in orbit hits with over 150 times the kinetic energy of a high-powered rifle round.

**(c)** The relative velocity can exceed the orbital velocity because two objects can be on *different* orbits — different inclinations, different directions — so their velocity *vectors* differ even if both have the same *speed*. For two objects both moving at ~7.5 km/s, the relative velocity is the vector difference: if they're moving in the same direction, it's small; if perpendicular, it's $\sqrt{2}\times7.5 \approx 10.6$ km/s; if head-on (opposite directions), it's the sum, $2\times7.45 = \mathbf{14.9\ km/s}$ maximum for circular LEO at 800 km. Most collisions are somewhere in between (~10 km/s typical), because orbits with a range of inclinations cross at large angles.

**(d)** Even small debris is lethal because at ~10 km/s, kinetic energy ($\propto v^2$) is so high that a gram-scale fragment carries bullet-to-grenade energy — enough to penetrate spacecraft walls and destroy critical systems. The 1–10 cm population is the worst: too small to track (so you can't dodge it) but large enough to be lethal. A **Whipple shield** defends against this: it's a thin outer "bumper" layer spaced some distance from the main hull, so an incoming hypervelocity fragment strikes the bumper and *shatters/vaporizes* into a spray of smaller, lower-density debris, which then spreads out over the gap and is absorbed by the main wall — converting one lethal projectile into a survivable cloud. It works against small debris but not against large trackable objects (which you must dodge) — so the strategy is: shield the small stuff, track-and-dodge the big stuff, and hope the medium stuff (1–10 cm) misses you.

### 2 — The cascade condition

**(a)** The critical condition for a self-sustaining cascade: **each collision must produce, on average, more than one subsequent collision** (from its fragments) before those fragments decay — the debris-cascade analog of an epidemic's basic reproduction number $R_0$. If $R_0 < 1$, each collision's fragments cause fewer than one further collision on average, and the process dies out. If $R_0 > 1$, each collision causes more than one further collision, the fragment population grows exponentially, and the cascade is self-sustaining (the Kessler syndrome). The threshold is $R_0 = 1$.

**(b)** The collision rate grows as roughly the *square* of the number of objects because a collision requires a *pair* of objects, and the number of possible pairs among $n$ objects is $n(n-1)/2 \approx n^2/2$. Double the population and you roughly quadruple the number of pairs that could collide, hence (at fixed density/geometry) roughly quadruple the collision rate. This quadratic growth is what makes the cascade accelerate: more objects → many more pairs → more collisions → more fragments → even more objects.

**(c)** Once triggered at high altitude, the cascade cannot be stopped because (i) you cannot recall or capture the millions of fragments already created; (ii) you cannot prevent them from colliding — they're on independent orbits crossing each other's paths; and (iii) the only natural removal mechanism, atmospheric drag, is negligible at high altitude (decay times of centuries to millennia above ~800 km), so fragments aren't removed faster than they're created. Contrast a **nuclear chain reaction**, which can be damped by inserting control rods that absorb neutrons and drop $R_0$ below 1 — there's a physical "brake." The Kessler cascade has no brake: no control rod, no way to remove fragments faster than they multiply, and drag (the only natural sink) doesn't reach the dangerous altitudes. It simply runs until the orbit is saturated, over centuries.

**(d)** Drag is central because it's the *removal* term in the cascade balance — the thing that competes against fragment *creation*. Whether an altitude band cascades depends on whether creation (collisions) outpaces removal (drag-induced decay). At low altitude, drag is strong, decay is fast (years), so debris is removed before it can cascade — $R_0$ stays low. At high altitude, drag is negligible, decay takes centuries, debris accumulates, and $R_0$ can exceed 1. **The cascade condition is literally a race between collision-creation and drag-removal, and drag only wins down low.** This is the direct continuation of the decay story: there, drag was a killer (deorbiting Skylab); here, drag is the savior, the only free janitor — and it only reaches the low orbits.

### 3 — Altitude and decay

**(a)** Debris at 400 km is far less of a long-term problem than at 1000 km because atmospheric drag deorbits 400-km debris within ~1 year (it burns up harmlessly), while 1000-km debris persists for *~1000+ years*. Both are equally lethal in a collision, but the 400-km debris *removes itself* quickly, so it can't accumulate or cascade — the atmosphere continuously cleans that altitude. The 1000-km debris stays essentially forever on human timescales, accumulating toward cascade. **Danger for the commons is about persistence, not just lethality, and persistence is set by drag, which is set by altitude.**

**(b)** Ranking by long-term commons danger (most to least dangerous): **1200 km > 800 km > 36,000 km (GEO) > 350 km.** Reasoning: 1200 km has millennia-long persistence *and* significant traffic → worst. 800 km has century-plus persistence and is extremely crowded (sun-synchronous) → very bad. GEO has no drag at all (debris persists indefinitely) but is a single thin ring with graveyard-orbit disposal and less crossing-collision geometry → serious but more manageable, and lower collision rates. 350 km has strong drag (debris decays in months) → least dangerous, self-cleaning. (One could argue GEO above 800 km given its infinite persistence; the ranking hinges on weighting persistence vs. traffic density and collision geometry — a defensible reshuffle of the middle two.)

**(c)** The cruel irony: the ~800 km band is the prime real estate for **sun-synchronous orbits** — Earth observation, imaging, weather, reconnaissance — because that's where the sun-synchronous condition and useful imaging altitudes coincide, so it's extremely crowded with valuable satellites. But 800 km is *also* high enough that drag is nearly useless (century-plus persistence), so debris there accumulates and cascades. **The very orbits we most want to use are the ones the atmosphere can't clean**, so our most valuable band is our most vulnerable to permanent ruin. We crowded our junk into exactly the altitude where junk lasts longest.

**(d)** We can't rely on drag to clean GEO because at 36,000 km there is *no* atmosphere — zero drag, so debris persists indefinitely (far longer even than high-LEO debris). Instead, GEO satellites at end of life are boosted into a **graveyard orbit** a few hundred km above the GEO ring, moving them out of the crowded operational belt (a small upward Δv, far cheaper than the enormous Δv needed to deorbit from GEO all the way to the atmosphere). It's disposal-by-relocation, not removal — a stopgap that works because the graveyard is currently uncrowded, but it doesn't eliminate the junk, just shelves it.

### 4 — The tragedy of the commons

**(a)** The tragedy's structure (Hardin): a shared, finite, un-owned resource; actors who each capture the full *private* benefit of using it while the *cost* of degrading it is spread across all users; so each actor rationally over-uses, and the sum ruins the resource. Mapped onto orbital debris: the **shared resource** is usable orbital space (slots, altitude bands, and the low-debris condition of the commons); the **private benefit** is the revenue/capability an operator gets from its satellite; the **shared cost** is the increased collision risk and commons-degradation that the operator's debris imposes on *all* orbit-users. Each operator gets all the benefit of launching and bears only a sliver of the debris cost, so each rationally under-invests in deorbiting/passivation, and the sum degrades the commons toward Kessler.

**(b)** Debris is a **negative externality**: a cost an actor imposes on others without paying it themselves (like factory pollution imposed on downstream residents). Externalities lead to over-production of the harmful thing because the producer doesn't factor the un-paid cost into their decisions — since deorbiting/not-polluting costs *them* money while the benefit accrues to *everyone*, they rationally produce more debris than is socially optimal. The market "price" of launching debris-generating satellites is too low because it excludes the cost imposed on others, so too many get launched and too few get cleaned up.

**(c)** No one is the villain in 2009 because the outcome was structural, not a moral failure. Cosmos 2251 was a dead satellite that no one was *obligated* to remove (no rule required it, no one paid to), so it sat there — a rational (cost-avoiding) non-action by its operator. Iridium 33 was doing its job. Neither operator was negligent by the standards and rules of the time; the collision arose because the *structure* (no deorbit obligation, no traffic authority, incomplete tracking) permitted a dead object to persist and cross a working satellite's path with no one empowered or incentivized to prevent it. **Blaming individuals misses the point: change the individuals and the same structure produces the same tragedy.**

**(d)** *(Example.)* **Overfishing** of the open ocean: fish stocks are a shared, finite, un-owned resource; each fishing fleet captures the full benefit of the fish it catches while the cost (stock depletion) is spread across all fleets and future fishers; so each rationally over-fishes, and stocks collapse (as many have). The structural analogy to orbital debris is exact: private benefit of use, shared cost of degradation, no owner, no enforceable limits → over-exploitation and collapse. (Climate change, groundwater depletion, and antibiotic overuse share the same structure.)

### 5 — Space law

**(a)** The 1967 Outer Space Treaty's debris-relevant principles: **non-appropriation** (no nation may claim sovereignty over any part of space or celestial bodies — space is "the province of all mankind"); **state responsibility** (states are responsible for their national space activities, *including those of private companies*, and must authorize and supervise them); and **liability** (states are internationally liable for damage caused by their space objects, elaborated in the 1972 Liability Convention).

**(b)** The paradox: the **non-appropriation** principle was a noble anti-colonial safeguard — it prevented powerful nations from claiming the Moon or orbital territory the way empires claimed continents, keeping space open to all. But "no one may own space" is *precisely* the condition that creates a tragedy of the commons: because no one owns orbital slots or altitude bands, there are no property rights, no owner with an incentive to protect the resource, no mechanism to price its use or exclude over-users. **The principle that keeps space free (no ownership) is the same principle that leaves it unprotected (no ownership)** — the treaty's greatest virtue and the tragedy's root cause are one and the same.

**(c)** The Liability Convention has almost never been successfully invoked for debris because (i) **proving fault and causation** for a hypervelocity collision is extraordinarily hard — after two satellites vaporize into thousands of fragments, establishing exactly whose object hit whose, and whose fault it was, is a forensic and legal nightmare; (ii) most debris is **un-attributed** (you can't prove which nation's fragment damaged your satellite); (iii) states are **reluctant to set precedents** that could later be used against their own vast debris populations (the 2009 collision produced no claim partly because neither the US nor Russia wanted to open that door); and (iv) the Convention addresses discrete damage events, not the *gradual, collective* degradation that is the real debris problem. Formal liability exists on paper but is nearly unusable in practice.

**(d)** Three inadequacies for the 2020s: (i) **No property rights or externality pricing** — the anti-appropriation principle blocks any market mechanism to align incentives, so the commons stays un-priced and over-used. (ii) **Weak/absent enforcement** — debris-mitigation "guidelines" (25-year deorbit) are voluntary, there's no global space-traffic authority with power to compel, and liability is nearly un-invokable. (iii) **It didn't anticipate the commercial/megaconstellation era** — written for a handful of government satellites operated by two superpowers, it has no framework for thousands of commercial satellites, no coordination of megaconstellations, and no regulation of debris-generating anti-satellite tests against one's own satellites.

### 6 — Ostrom's rebuttal

**(a)** Elinor Ostrom challenged Hardin by showing, *empirically*, that his conclusion (commons are inevitably doomed without privatization or top-down state control) is often false: real communities around the world — fisheries, forests, irrigation systems, alpine pastures — have successfully governed shared resources for generations through **self-organized collective governance**, without either privatizing the resource or submitting to external government control. She demonstrated that Hardin described only *one* possible outcome of a badly-governed commons, not a law of nature, and that a *third* path (community self-governance) exists between privatization and state control.

**(b)** Three of Ostrom's design principles and their difficulty for orbit: (i) **Clearly defined boundaries** (who may use the resource) — *hard* for orbit: the "users" are all of humanity's spacefaring nations and companies, and defining who's in the governance community is politically fraught. (ii) **Monitoring** (usage and violations are observed) — *moderately feasible but hard*: we can track large debris and launches, but attributing degradation, monitoring compliance globally, and catching "cheaters" is technically and politically difficult. (iii) **Graduated sanctions** (violators face escalating penalties) — *very hard*: there's no global authority to impose sanctions on sovereign nations, and enforcement against a superpower or a major commercial actor is nearly impossible. (Others: collective-choice participation, conflict resolution, recognition of the right to organize — all strained by the sovereign, competitive, global nature of the actors.)

**(c)** The orbital commons is a *particularly hard* case because: the "community" consists of **sovereign nations and competing corporations** (not a village of neighbors who see each other daily and share norms); there is **no overarching authority** to enforce rules on sovereigns; **boundaries are ill-defined** (orbit is global, and one nation's debris threatens all); **monitoring is technically hard** (much debris is untrackable and unattributable); the **stakes are geopolitical and commercial** (national security, huge profits), raising the temptation to defect; and the **community is enormous and impersonal**, lacking the repeated face-to-face interaction and shared identity that make village-scale self-governance work. A village fishery has a small, stable, interdependent community with shared norms and local enforcement; the orbital commons has none of these.

**(d)** *(Either direction is defensible.)* One strong answer: Ostrom's work supports *cautious optimism*, because it refutes the fatalism that the debris crisis is an inevitable tragedy — it proves that commons *can* be governed, and points concretely at *what* is needed (defined boundaries, monitoring, graduated sanctions, collective-choice participation). The path is identified, not just lamented. But the optimism is cautious, because the orbital commons violates almost every condition that makes Ostrom-style governance succeed (sovereign actors, no enforcement authority, hard monitoring, geopolitical stakes), so success is possible but far from assured — it would require deliberate institution-building against strong defection incentives. Ostrom turns "we're doomed" into "we could succeed if we build the right institutions" — which is more hopeful and more demanding at once. *(A well-argued pessimistic answer, emphasizing how thoroughly orbit violates Ostrom's conditions, is equally acceptable.)*

### 7 — Design a debris-mitigation regime (sample essay)

*(There is no single correct regime; a strong answer is specific, integrates engineering, economics, and law, and is honest about its weakest point. One possibility:)*

A realistic regime must combine **mandates, pricing, and cleanup funding**, tied to the one real point of leverage that exists: launch licensing. **Engineering mandates:** condition every launch license (issued by national regulators — the FCC, ESA states, etc.) on binding, enforceable requirements: mandatory passivation, a reserved deorbit capability, and deorbit within 5 years of end-of-mission for LEO (or graveyard disposal for GEO), with a substantial performance bond posted at launch and forfeited if the operator fails to dispose properly. This turns voluntary guidelines into licensing conditions with financial teeth, using the fact that every operator must be licensed *somewhere.* **Economic mechanism:** impose an **orbital-use fee** proportional to each satellite's expected debris risk — a function of its mass, altitude (persistence), and maneuverability — so the debris externality appears on the operator's balance sheet (a Pigouvian tax). This incentivizes operators to choose self-cleaning orbits, deorbit promptly, and design for maneuverability, and the revenue funds the next piece. **Cleanup as a public good:** since no operator will remove debris it didn't create, fund **active debris removal** collectively from the orbital-use fees, prioritizing the highest-risk objects (large derelicts in the 800–1000 km cascade-prone bands, whose removal most reduces cascade probability). **Institutional structure:** a coordinating body (evolving from existing space-situational-awareness cooperation) provides tracking, conjunction warnings, and traffic coordination, and administers the fee-and-cleanup fund; buy-in is achieved by tying market access (launch and spectrum licensing, which nations *do* control) to participation, so that defectors lose commercial access. **The weakest point, honestly:** enforcement against major sovereign actors (a superpower or a nation determined to conduct anti-satellite tests) — the regime works by leveraging licensing and market access, but a determined state actor with its own launch capability and domestic market can defect, and there is no authority to compel it. The regime raises the cost of defection and shrinks the defecting coalition, but cannot eliminate defection by the most powerful actors — which is the same fundamental limit that afflicts all global-commons governance, from climate to fisheries.

### 8 — The anti-satellite problem (sample essay)

**(a)** Anti-satellite tests are among the worst debris events because they deliberately create a hypervelocity collision — a missile striking a satellite at kinetic energies that shatter it into thousands of trackable fragments and hundreds of thousands of untrackable ones — typically at high altitudes (the 2007 Fengyun-1C test was at ~865 km) where the debris persists for centuries and spreads into a shell threatening everything in that band. A single test can increase the tracked-debris population by thousands of objects in an instant (Fengyun-1C created ~3,000, the single largest debris event in history), polluting a valuable orbital band for longer than any nation has existed — an enormous, irreversible, involuntary imposition on every other space user.

**(b)** *The case FOR a binding ban:* the debris threatens all spacefarers (including the testing nation's own assets and the ISS), persists for centuries, and provides limited military value relative to its catastrophic and permanent commons cost; a ban is a clear positive-sum agreement that protects a resource everyone depends on. *The strongest case AGAINST:* (i) **national security** — anti-satellite capability may be a strategic deterrent, and a nation may be unwilling to forgo demonstrating or developing it while rivals possess it; (ii) **verification** — a test ban is hard to verify and distinguishes awkwardly between destructive tests and non-destructive ones, and a nation might fear others cheating; (iii) **fairness** — a ban may entrench the advantage of nations that *already* tested and developed the capability, freezing out later entrants (analogous to non-proliferation critiques), and could be seen as the space-powers pulling the ladder up; (iv) **scope** — banning only *destructive* tests doesn't address the underlying capability, which can be developed by other means.

**(c)** *(Either position is defensible; what counts is the reasoning.)* One position: on balance, the stronger case favors a binding ban on *destructive, debris-generating* tests specifically (not necessarily the capability itself), because the debris cost is uniquely catastrophic, permanent, and imposed on all — a category of harm distinct from ordinary military competition — and because non-destructive tests can demonstrate capability without polluting the commons, partly addressing the security objection. The fairness and verification concerns are real but manageable: the ban targets an *act* (destructive testing) that is observable (the debris is trackable and attributable), and the "ladder-pulling" concern is outweighed by the fact that continued testing threatens the very orbits everyone including new entrants needs. The growing set of nations adopting voluntary moratoria on destructive tests suggests this is politically achievable and is the most tractable single win available for the commons. *(A well-argued opposing position, weighting security and verification more heavily, is equally acceptable.)*

### 9 — Is space law adequate? (sample essay)

*(Either side is defensible if the opposing view is steelmanned. One essay, arguing inadequacy:)*

**Steelman of "adequate, needs only enforcement":** The Outer Space Treaty's principles are sound and flexible — state responsibility, liability, and the province-of-mankind ethos provide a genuine foundation, and much of the debris problem could be addressed by *enforcing* existing debris-mitigation guidelines through national licensing (which is beginning to happen, e.g., the FCC's 5-year deorbit rule). On this view, we don't need a new treaty (which would take decades to negotiate and might be weaker); we need to operationalize what we have. This is a serious argument, and the licensing-leverage point is correct as far as it goes. **But it is ultimately insufficient**, for a structural reason: the treaty framework establishes *no property rights and no externality pricing*, and the debris problem is fundamentally an externality problem. Enforcement of deorbit guidelines helps at the margin, but without a mechanism to *price* the debris an operator creates — to make the cost of degrading the commons appear on the operator's balance sheet — the underlying incentive misalignment remains, and national licensing is fragmented (each nation sets its own rules, operators can forum-shop for lax jurisdictions, and the guidelines address only *future* debris, not the existing derelicts driving the cascade). Moreover, the framework has no answer at all for two critical gaps: **anti-satellite tests** (legal under the treaty) and **funding active removal** of existing debris (a public good no one is obligated to provide). These are not enforcement gaps; they are *design* gaps — things the 1967 framework simply doesn't contemplate. So while the treaty needn't be *replaced* (its core principles are worth keeping), it requires major *augmentation*: binding debris limits, externality pricing, collective cleanup funding, and anti-satellite regulation — none of which "better enforcement of existing rules" can supply, because the rules for them don't exist. The framework is a necessary foundation that is, by itself, fundamentally inadequate to the crisis. *(The opposing essay — arguing adequacy-plus-enforcement, steelmanning the inadequacy view first — is equally acceptable.)*

### 10 — Synthesis and finale (sample capstone essay)

**This chapter closes the story of orbits by completing an arc that runs from a single ellipse to the governance of a shared sky — from "where things can go" to "whether we can share where they go."** The earlier chapters made us masters of trajectory: the two-body orbit and its conserved quantities, the perturbations we learned to tune into tools, the counterintuitive rendezvous where we slow down to catch up, the interplanetary reach that steals energy from planets, and the unsolvable three-body frontier with its Lagrange points and chaos. Across those chapters we became extraordinarily good at putting things where we want them in space. This chapter confronts the consequence: we have become so good at *using* orbit that we are on the verge of *ruining* it, and the mastery of trajectory that got us here is powerless against the problem it created — because the problem is not about trajectory at all.

**The debris crisis is simultaneously a physics problem and a governance problem, and that dual nature is the chapter's core.** The physics is pure orbital mechanics: debris is lethal because orbital speed is 7.5 km/s and kinetic energy scales as velocity squared, so a fleck of paint hits like a bullet; debris persists because drag can't reach above ~600 km, so the atmosphere — our only free janitor — leaves the best orbits uncleaned; collisions cascade because each fragment is both victim and source, and above a critical density the Kessler syndrome runs away like a chain reaction with no brake, for centuries. And the mitigation tools — deorbit burns, graveyard orbits, collision avoidance, active removal — are all astrodynamics. The engineer who learned the orbital chapters understands the disease and the cure completely.

**But the disease has no engineering cure, because it is a tragedy of the commons — an incentive problem, not a technology problem.** Orbit is a finite, shared, un-owned resource; each operator captures the private benefit of using it while imposing the cost of degrading it on everyone; so each rationally under-invests in protection, and the sum slides toward cascade. Debris is a negative externality — orbital pollution — and the polluter doesn't pay, so there's too much of it. We know exactly how to prevent debris; we are neither obligated nor incentivized to. And the governance we have is a visionary 1967 treaty written for two superpowers, establishing no property rights, no enforcement, no externality pricing, straining under a commercial reality of thousands of operators — its noblest principle (no one owns space) being the very root of the tragedy (a thing no one owns is a thing no one protects). Yet Ostrom complicates the pessimism decisively: the tragedy of the commons is not a law of nature but a particular institutional failure, and real communities do govern shared resources through self-organized rules, monitoring, and sanctions. The orbital commons is a hard case — sovereign, competitive, un-policeable actors — but the paths exist (binding deorbit rules, externality pricing, traffic management, collective cleanup, test bans), and whether we walk them before a cascade forecloses them is genuinely open.

**The through-line transforms, at the end, from efficiency into stewardship, and this is the deepest note of the arc.** *Never carry anything through a Δv it does not need to experience* began as a rule for saving payload — drop the dead tank. It ends here as *never leave in orbit anything that does not need to be there* — drop your dead satellite, don't foul the commons. The dead satellite and spent stage are the ultimate dead mass, coasting endlessly through the shared sky, and the cost is borne not by one rocket's payload but by all humanity's access to space and by the generations to come. That a study of astrodynamics ends not with an equation but with a question of collective responsibility is exactly right: **spaceflight is done by human institutions, and the spacecraft engineer of the coming decades cannot only be a master of trajectories but must be a citizen of the commons** — aware that every design choice (passivate or not, reserve deorbit fuel or not, choose a self-cleaning orbit or a persistent one) is a small act of governance, a contribution to or a theft from a resource held in trust for all. The orbit you fill is not yours. The orbital chapters taught you to navigate the heavens; their final chapter teaches that navigating them well is not only a technical achievement but a moral and institutional one — and that the sky, like every commons, is kept only by those who choose to keep it.$l13_master$,
