with q as (
  select $l13_quiz$[{"type": "mcq", "prompt": "A 1-gram fleck of paint in low Earth orbit can crater the window of a spacecraft. Why is something so small so dangerous?", "options": ["Paint is chemically corrosive to spacecraft glass", "At orbital speeds the closing velocity is ~10 km/s, and kinetic energy scales as v\u00b2, so even a gram-scale fragment carries the energy of a bullet or grenade fragment", "The vacuum makes the paint expand and shatter the glass", "Static electricity in orbit magnifies small impacts"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Kinetic energy is \u00bdmv\u00b2, and orbital velocity in LEO is ~7.5 km/s, so two objects on different orbits can close at up to ~15 km/s (typically ~10 km/s). Because energy grows as the square of speed, a 1-gram fragment at 10 km/s carries ~50 kJ \u2014 more than a hand-grenade fragment \u2014 and a 10-gram bolt carries ~300\u00d7 the energy of a rifle bullet. This is why a lost bolt is a hypervelocity bomb and a paint chip has chipped Shuttle and ISS windows. The 1\u201310 cm population is the deadliest: lethal, yet too small to track and dodge, which is why hulls carry Whipple shields against it.", "id": "q1"}, {"type": "mcq", "prompt": "What is the defining condition of the Kessler syndrome \u2014 the point at which orbital debris becomes a self-sustaining cascade?", "options": ["When any two satellites collide for the first time", "When, on average, each collision causes more than one further collision before its fragments decay \u2014 an orbital analog of an epidemic's R\u2080 exceeding 1", "When the total number of satellites exceeds ten thousand", "When atmospheric drag stops working at any altitude"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The cascade is a chain reaction. Each collision produces fragments that cross other objects' paths and can cause further collisions. The threshold is whether each collision causes, on average, more than one subsequent collision before drag removes the fragments \u2014 exactly the basic reproduction number R\u2080 of an epidemic. If R\u2080 < 1 the process fizzles (drag removes debris faster than it cascades); if R\u2080 > 1 it runs away exponentially, fed by its own fragments. The collision rate itself grows as the square of the population, because collisions require pairs and the number of pairs among n objects grows as n\u00b2/2 \u2014 so doubling the population roughly quadruples the collision opportunities.", "id": "q2"}, {"type": "mcq", "prompt": "Debris at 400 km and debris at 1000 km are equally lethal in a collision, yet the 1000 km debris is far worse for the commons. Why?", "options": ["The 1000 km debris moves faster and hits harder", "Atmospheric drag deorbits 400 km debris within a few years, but is negligible above ~600 km, so 1000 km debris persists for centuries \u2014 long enough to accumulate and cascade", "There are simply more satellites at 400 km", "The 400 km debris is easier to track"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Danger for the commons is about persistence, not just lethality, and persistence is set by drag, which is set by altitude. Below ~600 km, drag deorbits debris within years to a couple of decades \u2014 the atmosphere is a free, self-cleaning janitor \u2014 so fragments decay before they can cascade and R\u2080 stays low. Above ~800 km, drag is negligible and debris persists for centuries to millennia, accumulating toward cascade with no natural cleanup. The cruel irony is that the ~800\u20131000 km band is exactly where the most valuable satellites live (sun-synchronous imaging), so our most-wanted orbits are the ones the atmosphere cannot clean \u2014 and the most vulnerable to permanent ruin.", "id": "q3"}, {"type": "mcq", "prompt": "Economically, why does low Earth orbit keep filling with debris even though the mitigation techniques (deorbit, passivation, avoidance) are well understood?", "options": ["The techniques do not actually work in practice", "It is a tragedy of the commons: each operator captures the private benefit of using orbit while the cost of the debris it creates is spread across all users \u2014 a negative externality the polluter does not pay", "Governments have banned the mitigation techniques", "There is not enough tracking data to know where the debris is"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The crisis is not a technology or knowledge problem but an incentive problem. Orbit is a finite, shared, un-owned resource; the benefit of launching a satellite is private (the operator's revenue), while the cost of degrading the commons (increased collision risk for everyone) is shared, so each operator bears only a sliver of it. Deorbiting, passivating, and reserving disposal fuel all cost the individual operator while the benefit accrues to all \u2014 so the rational operator under-invests in protection, and the sum of those rational choices degrades the commons toward Kessler. Debris is a negative externality \u2014 orbital pollution \u2014 and because the polluter does not pay, there is too much of it. No one is the villain; the failure is structural.", "id": "q4"}, {"type": "mcq", "prompt": "The 1967 Outer Space Treaty is often described as visionary, yet its central principle is also blamed for the debris tragedy. What is the paradox?", "options": ["It banned all commercial spaceflight, so only governments can clean up debris", "Its non-appropriation principle \u2014 no nation may own any part of space \u2014 keeps space free for all, but 'no one owns it' is exactly the condition that leaves it with no property rights and no owner to protect it", "It requires every nation to deorbit its satellites, which is too expensive", "It gives a single global authority total control over orbit"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Outer Space Treaty's non-appropriation principle was a noble anti-colonial safeguard: no nation may claim the Moon or an orbital band the way empires claimed continents, keeping space the 'province of all mankind.' But 'no one may own space' is precisely the tragedy-of-the-commons condition \u2014 no property rights, no orbital slots anyone owns, no owner with an incentive to protect the resource, no mechanism to price its use. The treaty's greatest virtue and the tragedy's root cause are one and the same. Compounding it, enforcement is weak (debris guidelines are voluntary, liability is nearly un-invokable), and the framework never anticipated thousands of commercial operators or debris-generating ASAT tests against one's own satellites.", "id": "q5"}, {"type": "open", "prompt": "Explain the Kessler syndrome in full: the cascade logic, the critical condition, why the collision rate grows as the square of the population, why altitude is decisive, and why \u2014 once triggered at high altitude \u2014 it cannot be stopped.", "rubric": "Cascade logic: a collision (or explosion, or ASAT test) creates a cloud of fragments; each fragment is on its own orbit crossing other objects at hypervelocity; some strike other satellites or debris; each strike shatters its target into thousands more fragments; those cause more strikes \u2014 a chain reaction structurally identical to a nuclear reaction or an epidemic. Critical condition: whether, on average, each collision causes MORE THAN ONE subsequent collision before its fragments decay \u2014 the orbital analog of an epidemic's basic reproduction number R\u2080. If R\u2080 < 1 the process fizzles (drag removes debris faster than it cascades); if R\u2080 > 1 it runs away exponentially, self-sustaining, growing even with no new launches. n\u00b2 growth: collisions require PAIRS of objects, and the number of pairs among n objects is n(n\u22121)/2 \u2248 n\u00b2/2, so doubling the population roughly quadruples the collision opportunities \u2014 and each collision adds fragments to n, raising the rate again (quadratic feedback). Altitude is decisive because drag is the only natural removal term: below ~600 km drag deorbits debris within years, so fragments decay before cascading and R\u2080 stays low (the ISS at ~400 km is relatively self-cleaning); above ~800 km drag is negligible and debris persists for centuries to millennia, so debris accumulates and R\u2080 can exceed 1 \u2014 and this is exactly the crowded, valuable band (sun-synchronous ~800\u20131000 km). The cascade condition is literally a race between collision-creation and drag-removal, and drag only wins down low. Once triggered at high altitude it cannot be stopped because (i) the millions of fragments already created cannot be recalled or captured; (ii) they cannot be prevented from colliding, being on independent crossing orbits; and (iii) the only natural sink, atmospheric drag, is negligible at those altitudes, so fragments are not removed faster than they multiply. Unlike a nuclear chain reaction (dampable with control rods) or an epidemic (slowable with vaccines), the Kessler cascade has no brake \u2014 it simply runs for centuries until the orbit is saturated and unusable. Studies suggest the ~800\u20131000 km bands may already be above the critical density, meaning a slow cascade may already be under way.", "id": "q6"}, {"type": "open", "prompt": "Explain why orbital debris is a 'tragedy of the commons,' why the problem is one of governance rather than engineering, and how Elinor Ostrom's work complicates the pessimism. Name at least two concrete paths toward governing the orbital commons.", "rubric": "Tragedy of the commons (Hardin): a shared, finite, un-owned resource; each actor captures the full PRIVATE benefit of using it while the COST of degrading it is spread across all users, so each rationally over-uses and under-protects, and the sum ruins the resource. Mapped onto orbit: the shared resource is usable orbital space and the low-debris condition of the commons; the private benefit is the operator's satellite revenue/capability; the shared cost is the increased collision risk and commons-degradation imposed on all users. Each operator therefore under-invests in deorbiting, passivation, and disposal fuel (all of which cost the individual while benefiting everyone), and the sum slides toward Kessler. Debris is a NEGATIVE EXTERNALITY \u2014 orbital pollution \u2014 and because the polluter does not pay, there is too much of it. This is why it is a GOVERNANCE problem, not an engineering one: the mitigation tools all exist and are understood, but no one is obligated or incentivized to use them; the fix requires rules, property rights, pricing, treaties, and enforcement, not cleverer equations. And the governance we have \u2014 the 1967 Outer Space Treaty \u2014 establishes no property rights (its noble 'no appropriation' principle is the very tragedy condition), has weak/voluntary enforcement, and never anticipated thousands of commercial operators or ASAT tests. Ostrom's rebuttal: Elinor Ostrom (2009 Nobel) showed EMPIRICALLY that Hardin's fatalism is often wrong \u2014 real communities govern shared resources (fisheries, forests, irrigation, pastures) successfully WITHOUT privatization or top-down control, through self-organized rules, monitoring, graduated sanctions, and collective decision-making. So the tragedy is not a law of nature but a particular institutional failure, and the question becomes whether we can BUILD the institutions to govern orbit before a cascade forecloses the option. Orbit is a hard case (sovereign, competing actors; ill-defined boundaries; hard monitoring; no shared enforcement \u2014 unlike a small village fishery), but not hopeless. Concrete paths (any two): binding debris-mitigation rules tied to launch/spectrum licensing with financial penalties (turning voluntary 5\u201325-year deorbit guidelines into enforceable conditions); pricing the externality via an orbital-use fee proportional to mass, altitude, and maneuverability (a Pigouvian tax that puts the cost on the operator's balance sheet); space-traffic management like air-traffic control; collectively-funded active debris removal as a public good; and binding ASAT-test bans building on existing voluntary moratoria. A good answer notes these all require collective action among competing sovereigns \u2014 the same fundamental difficulty as climate and fisheries \u2014 but that Ostrom shows the paths can lead somewhere.", "id": "q7"}]$l13_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
